{"id":"9d404c56-f7aa-4abf-9f05-aaf4f559df82","object":"chat.completion","created":1793336473,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Baseline fails as expected (feature absent). Now inspecting the rest of logger.py and package init.\n\n```python\nupdate_plan([{\"title\":\"Read required source files\",\"status\":\"doing\"},{\"title\":\"Run baseline pytest\",\"status\":\"done\"},{\"title\":\"Implement environ.py config\",\"status\":\"todo\"},{\"title\":\"Implement logger.py formatter budget\",\"status\":\"todo\"},{\"title\":\"Implement stats_logger.py extra flag\",\"status\":\"todo\"},{\"title\":\"Run final pytest and produce patch/logs\",\"status\":\"todo\"}])\na = read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 68, 160)\nprint(a['content'])\nb = read_file(\"deepinfer/__init__.py\")\nprint(b['content'][:2000])\n```","reasoning_content":"Baseline is done (50 failed, 10 passed expected since feature missing). Wait — baseline_done=1 already. But the failures include `test_invalid_budget[True] - TypeError: NewLine...` — this is fine, the initial version lacks the feature.\n\nPlan: update plan statuses for read and baseline done, then make edits.\n\nLet me read remaining details I need: logger.py rest (lines 68-120), environ.py sections around refresh functions (already have rearm/rearm_all). Let me read logger.py lines 68-130 fully to see _setup_logger and any get_logger function.\n\nActually I have evidence of lines 75-90 (_setup_logger) and 96-107 (init_logger). I need to know full logger.py structure to edit precisely. Let me read lines 68-130.\n\nThen edits:\n\n1) environ.py: add descriptor factory for int with min bound, or use custom lambda:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_min(*, minimum: int = 0) -> EnvVar[int]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int:\n            if v is None:\n                raise ValueError(\"must be set\")  # hmm, but need None allowed\n```\n\nRequirement: unset -> None; set -> int >= 32; empty string, unparseable, or <32 -> ValueError with var name in text. Note var name is added by descriptor wrapper anyway (`f\"{self.name}: {e}\"`), but also we should include it. The existing descriptor wrapper raises `type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. For empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError -> wrapper adds name. Good. But careful: the wrapper does `raise type(e)(...)` inside try/except TypeError — for ValueError that works.\n\nSo a factory:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_at_least(minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nNote empty string: os.getenv returns \"\" which is not None -> int(\"\") ValueError. Good.\n\nAdd to envs: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_at_least(32)` in Logging section.\n\nHmm, but tests may call something like `envs.rearm(\"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\")` and `rearm_all()`. Those exist already.\n\n2) logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _budget_middle(text: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(text) <= max_chars:\n        return text\n    remaining = max_chars - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return text[:head] + _OMITTED_MARKER + (text[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nWait example: budget 32, marker 15 chars (\"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9+3 = 15? \"<omitted>\" is 9 chars; plus 3 dots each side = 15). remaining = 17, head = ceil(17/2)=9, tail=8. text[:9] + marker + text[-8:] = total 9+15+8=32. Good.\n\nIn formatter __init__ accept max_message_chars keyword-only:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(...)\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nformat(): must do arg substitution first, without mutating record. Standard Formatter.format does record.message = record.getMessage() which mutates record; it sets record.message attribute — that's allowed? Requirement says don't rewrite shared record's msg, args, exc_info, stack_info. Setting record.message... In the original code, `super().format(record)` already sets record.message. Also super().format uses record.exc_info — it caches record.exc_text. Fine.\n\nApproach: \n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        # 参数替换先完成，保留原消息用于多行前缀对齐\n        full_message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = self._apply_budget(full_message, record)\n        if truncated is not full_message  # actually truncated != full_message\n```\nWe need to format with the truncated message without mutating record. Common trick: use a shallow copy of a LogRecord? But requirement: \"先完成 %s 等参数替换，再计算消息长度\" and \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args...\" Note keeping record.message set by super().format is presumably fine (original behavior). But if we truncate, we must not set record.msg to truncated.\n\nSimplest: build a copy of record via copy.copy, set msg to truncated and args=(), message=truncated. logging package has `logging.makeLogRecord` — could do:\n\n```python\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and record.keep_full_message is not True:\n            ...\n```\nRequirement: keep_full_message property exactly True. `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True` — use `is True`.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        limit = self.max_message_chars\n        if limit is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", False) is not True:\n            message = _truncate_message(message, limit)\n        # 保留原有多行前缀对齐：需要在副本上渲染，避免修改原记录\n        if message != record.getMessage():\n            record = _ShallowCopy...\n```\n\nCleaner: create a copy using logging.LogRecord-like copy:\n\n```python\nimport copy\n...\n        original_message = record.getMessage()\n        if limit is not None and ... :\n            truncated = _truncate_message(original_message, limit)\n        else:\n            truncated = original_message\n        if truncated is not original_message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = None\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        # multi-line prefix alignment uses record.message\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\n\nWait: super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() which for the copy = truncated. Good. And the copy doesn't mutate original. Also exc_info preserved on copy, exc_text caching happens on the copy only (fine; slight perf).\n\nBut careful: copy.copy of a log record with exc_info etc. works. Also `record.message` on the copy = truncated.\n\nNote requirement 5: \"沿用原有多行前缀、时间精度、颜色及异常处理\" — yes.\n\nIs `copy` import allowed? Allowed modules are collections, datetime, itertools, json, math, queue, random, re, stat, statistics, time, unicodedata — that's for action code imports. In source files I can import anything standard (copy is stdlib). But maybe evaluator restricts? \"可增加必要标准库导入\" — copy is fine. Alternatively avoid copy by temporarily setting and restoring, but copy is cleaner. Actually maybe simpler: use `logging.makeLogRecord`? That copies dict. Let me just use copy.\n\nHmm, careful about child logger / handler: `super().format(record)` also uses `record.exc_info` and sets `record.exc_text` — on copy, fine.\n\n3) _setup_logger: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Need import: `from deepinfer import envs`? logger.py currently imports nothing from deepinfer — check for circular imports: environ.py imports only stdlib, so `from deepinfer.environ import envs` in logger.py should be safe? But logger.py is imported by deepinfer/__init__ maybe. Let's check deepinfer/__init__.py to be safe. Simpler: `import deepinfer.environ as _environ`? Hmm. Actually `from deepinfer import envs` — deepinfer/__init__.py probably imports lots. Safer: `from deepinfer.environ import envs as _envs` — that triggers deepinfer/__init__ execution first (importing a submodule imports the package). Does deepinfer/__init__.py import logger? Let's read it.\n\nAlso \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code already guards _default_handler creation. But when re-initializing after refresh, the fmt is recreated and set on existing handler. Good.\n\nAlso requirement: `_setup_logger` should apply config on init; but note `init_logger` may be called at import time (stats_logger does `logger = init_logger(__name__)`). Where is _setup_logger called? Need to see logger.py lines 68-130 fully.\n\nLet me read logger.py 68-140, deepinfer/__init__.py, and maybe course tests for expected behaviors (tests visible in .course/test_logger.py — allowed to read tests? \"Edit only allowed files, never tests\" — reading tests is allowed and helpful). But careful: reading tests is fine.\n\nLet's do reads: logger.py 68-140, deepinfer/__init__.py, and .course/test_logger.py? The tests might be big; read to align error messages. Let's read logger.py rest and __init__.py first, then tests selectively.\n\nAlso need environ.py helper additions.\n\nPlan update and reads in one action."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"pro